<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>About · quiet ai</title>
<meta name="description" content="How quiet ai works: where the news comes from, how stories are grouped, ranked and labeled, and what the labels mean.">
<link rel="icon" href="data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 32 32'%3E%3Crect width='32' height='32' rx='6' fill='%23f6f3ec'/%3E%3Cpath d='M8 20h16M8 14h11' stroke='%23302d28' stroke-width='2.4' stroke-linecap='round'/%3E%3C/svg%3E">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ root }}static/style.css">
</head>
<body>
<div class="page about">

<header class="masthead">
  <div class="masthead-top">
    <p class="wordmark"><a href="{{ root }}">quiet ai</a></p>
    <p class="dateline"><a href="{{ root }}">Today's overview</a><span class="sep">·</span><span aria-current="page">About</span></p>
  </div>
</header>

<main class="prose">
<h1>How this site works</h1>
<p class="lede">Once a day, and updated every {{ a.every_hours }} hours, this page answers three questions: what happened in AI, how the press is framing it, and what people who build AI are actually making. It tries to do that in about five minutes of reading, without ads or noise.</p>

<nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
  <a href="#sources">Sources</a><a href="#steps">How a page is made</a><a href="#labels">Reading the labels</a><a href="#ranking">Ranking</a><a href="#periods">Time periods</a><a href="#limits">Limits</a><a href="#privacy">Privacy and cost</a><a href="#health">Source health</a>
</nav>

<section id="sources">
  <h2>Where the news comes from</h2>
  <p>Everything is collected from free, public sources. Nothing is behind a login or paid for.</p>
  <ul class="facts">
    <li><strong>{{ a.counts.outlets }} news outlets</strong> in {{ a.counts.countries }} countries and {{ a.counts.languages }} languages, read through their own RSS feeds.</li>
    <li><strong>{{ a.counts.gdelt }} more outlets</strong> with no usable feed ({{ a.gdelt_outlets|map(attribute="name")|join(", ") }}), read through the GDELT news index. For these only the headline and date are available.</li>
    <li><strong>{{ a.counts.aggregators }} editor-picked aggregators</strong> ({{ a.aggregators|join(" and ") }}), used only to tell which stories editors are leading with.</li>
    <li><strong>{{ a.counts.digests }} AI newsletters that summarize the day</strong> and <strong>{{ a.counts.commentary }} commentary newsletters</strong> from researchers, critics, policy writers and investors.</li>
    <li><strong>{{ a.counts.labs }} official AI lab blogs</strong>, attached to stories as the primary source.</li>
    <li><strong>Builder sources:</strong> new and fast-growing GitHub repositories, Hugging Face's trending models, datasets, spaces, daily papers and posts, Hacker News, new models on OpenRouter, Product Hunt's AI launches, and {{ a.counts.bluesky }} curated Bluesky accounts.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>We only keep each item's headline, a short summary (at most 500 characters), its link and its date. We never copy articles, never go around paywalls, and show no images. Every headline links to the original publisher. Sites whose robots.txt asks automated readers to stay away are left out, not worked around.{% if a.opt_out %} {{ a.opt_out|join(", ") }} {{ "asks" if a.opt_out|length == 1 else "ask" }} that its text not be used as input to AI systems, so its headlines are shown and counted but never labeled.{% endif %}</p>

  <details class="source-list">
    <summary>All {{ a.counts.outlets }} news outlets</summary>
    {% for g in a.groups %}
    <h3>{{ g.label }}</h3>
    <p>{% for o in g.outlets %}{{ o.name }} <span class="country">{{ o.country }}{% if o.language != "en" %}, {{ o.language }}{% endif %}</span>{% if not loop.last %}<span class="sep">·</span>{% endif %}{% endfor %}</p>
    {% endfor %}
    <h3>Through GDELT</h3>
    <p>{% for o in a.gdelt_outlets %}{{ o.name }} <span class="country">{{ o.country }}{% if o.type == "state-aligned" %}, state-aligned{% endif %}</span>{% if not loop.last %}<span class="sep">·</span>{% endif %}{% endfor %}</p>
    <h3>Newsletters</h3>
    <p>{{ a.newsletters|join(" · ") }}</p>
    <h3>Lab blogs</h3>
    <p>{{ a.labs|join(" · ") }}</p>
  </details>
</section>

<section id="steps">
  <h2>How a page is made</h2>
  <p>Every {{ a.every_hours }} hours the whole chain runs again:</p>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Collect.</strong> Every source is fetched politely: one request at a time per site, a clear name in every request, and no refetching of feeds that haven't changed.</li>
    <li><strong>Keep what is about AI.</strong> Items from AI-only sources are kept as they are. Others are checked for AI terms in five languages, and the unclear cases are decided by a small language model ({{ a.item_model }}). Headlines in other languages are translated into English for display; the original stays one hover away.</li>
    <li><strong>Group into stories.</strong> Each headline is turned into a numeric fingerprint of its meaning with a multilingual model that runs alongside the pipeline, not through an outside service ({{ a.embed_model.split("/")[-1] }}), so a Swedish and an English report of the same event land next to each other. Links count first: a Hacker News post or a Bluesky post that links to an article joins that article's story. Then headlines join the story they are closest to, but only when they are very close or share a name such as a company or a person. Stories stay open for {{ a.window_hours }} hours after their latest item.</li>
    <li><strong>Label each article.</strong> The language model reads only the headline and the start of the summary, never the outlet's name, and labels the angle, the framing and the stance (see below). It must quote the words that show its reasoning; a quote that is not really in the text is thrown out.</li>
    <li><strong>Rank.</strong> Stories are scored by how widely they are being picked up (see Ranking).</li>
    <li><strong>Summarize.</strong> For the top stories, a larger model ({{ a.story_model }}) writes a neutral one-line description of what happened, a sentence on how the coverage splits, and a sentence on what builders are doing. It also writes the one-line summary of the day at the top.</li>
    <li><strong>Build the page.</strong> The result is a plain static page, rebuilt from scratch every run.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section id="labels">
  <h2>Reading the labels</h2>
  <p>Each article gets three labels. They describe how the article talks about its subject, not whether it is right.</p>

  <h3>Angle</h3>
  <dl class="defs">
    <dt>Technical</dt><dd>Models, research, capabilities, benchmarks, tools, safety research. Framed as <em>hype</em> (bigger or nearer than shown), <em>grounded</em> (claims matched to evidence, or plain reporting) or <em>skeptical</em> (questioning the claims or the value).</dd>
    <dt>Political</dt><dd>Regulation, policy, geopolitics, chips and export controls, elections, courts. Framed as <em>pro-regulation</em>, <em>pro-acceleration</em>, <em>national competition</em> (a race between countries) or <em>neutral</em>.</dd>
    <dt>Economic</dt><dd>Funding, valuations, data centers, earnings, jobs, adoption. Framed as <em>boom</em>, <em>bubble</em>, <em>disruption concern</em> (harm to jobs or industries) or <em>neutral</em>.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h3>Stance</h3>
  <p>The article's attitude toward AI or the AI industry in that story. Plain factual reporting is neutral even when the news is good or bad for someone.</p>
  <ul class="stance-key">
    <li><span class="dot st-2"></span><strong>Strongly critical</strong>: alarmed or hostile.</li>
    <li><span class="dot st-1"></span><strong>Critical</strong>: worried or skeptical.</li>
    <li><span class="dot st0"></span><strong>Neutral</strong>: descriptive or balanced.</li>
    <li><span class="dot st1"></span><strong>Positive</strong>: optimistic.</li>
    <li><span class="dot st2"></span><strong>Enthusiastic</strong>: strongly positive or promotional.</li>
    <li><span class="dot stunclear"></span><strong>Unclear</strong>: the model wasn't confident, or couldn't point to words in the text. We would rather say so than guess.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The colors are deliberately not red and blue: stance here has nothing to do with left and right. The small bar on each story shows the mix across all of its coverage, and "Headlines by stance" lists one headline per outlet with the quoted words the label rests on, so you can judge for yourself.</p>
</section>

<section id="ranking">
  <h2>Ranking</h2>
  <p>A story ranks high when many different kinds of sources pick it up, not when one source shouts. Each signal is scaled from 0 to 1 across the stories in view, then weighted:</p>
  <table class="weights">
    <tbody>
    {% for w in a.weights %}<tr><td>{{ w.name }}</td><td class="num">{{ w.weight }}%</td></tr>{% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Counts like outlets and countries grow on a gentle curve, so 14 outlets stays well ahead of 2. Signals from a single platform, like Hacker News points or GitHub stars, are ranked rather than added up, so a viral thread can't outweigh broad coverage. Older activity counts for less, more gently in longer views.</p>
  <p><strong>Most mentioned</strong> lists the names (companies, people, products, places) that the most outlets mentioned in the period, each outlet counted once. <strong>Rising</strong> lists the builder projects gaining the most stars, likes or upvotes, grouped by what they are.</p>
</section>

<section id="periods">
  <h2>Time periods</h2>
  <p>You can look at the last {% for p in a.periods %}{{ p.label }}{% if not loop.last %}{{ ", " if loop.revindex > 2 else " or " }}{% endif %}{% endfor %}. Each view ranks stories only by what happened inside it. In the longer views, a story that went quiet and came back is shown as one.</p>
  {% if a.data_since %}<p>Collection started on {{ a.data_since_long }}. Until enough time has passed, the longer views say how much they actually cover.</p>{% endif %}
</section>

<section id="limits">
  <h2>Limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Labels are machine-made from a headline and a few lines of summary. A headline can frame a story differently from the article under it.</li>
    <li>The outlet list shapes what you see. It leans toward English-language and Western outlets, and some publishers (Reuters, AP, CNN, Chinese state media) are only visible through a secondary index with headlines alone.</li>
    <li>Grouping is automatic. Occasionally two related stories are split, or a loosely related piece joins a story.</li>
    <li>Social signals come from Bluesky and Hugging Face only. X is not included.</li>
    <li>GitHub stars can be bought. Obvious clone spam (the same project name from many accounts) is filtered out, but a single inflated repository can still show up in Rising.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="privacy">
  <h2>Privacy and cost</h2>
  <p>No ads, no cookies, no analytics, no tracking. The page loads nothing from other sites: no web fonts, no images, no scripts from elsewhere. The only script is a few lines for the filters, and the page works without it.</p>
  <p>The language models cost money. Spending is capped at ${{ a.budget|int }} a month; this month so far: ${{ "%.2f"|format(a.spend) }}. If the cap is reached, stories and links keep updating and the labels pause until the next month. Each label records the prompt version that produced it: currently relevance <code>{{ a.prompts.relevance }}</code>, article labels <code>{{ a.prompts.news }}</code>, project labels <code>{{ a.prompts.builder }}</code>, summaries <code>{{ a.prompts.summary }}</code> and the daily line <code>{{ a.prompts.weather }}</code>.</p>
</section>

<section id="health">
  <h2>Source health</h2>
  {% if a.health %}
  <p>Last collection, at {{ a.health.at_local }}: {{ a.health.ok }} of {{ a.health.total }} sources answered normally{% if a.health.failing %}, {{ a.health.failing|length }} did not{% endif %}.</p>
  {% if a.health.failing %}
  <ul class="health">
    {% for f in a.health.failing %}<li>{{ f.name }}: {{ f.status }}{% if f.error %} <span class="country">({{ f.error[:80] }})</span>{% endif %}</li>{% endfor %}
  </ul>
  {% endif %}
  {% else %}
  <p>No collection has run yet.</p>
  {% endif %}
</section>
</main>

<footer class="colophon">
  <p><a href="{{ root }}">Back to today's overview</a></p>
</footer>

</div>
</body>
</html>
